---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="skills — keel">
  <header class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">skills</span>
      <h2>Five skills, one direction of trust</h2>
      <p class="sub">Hooks capture, the CLI computes, skills interpret. Everything with
      judgment lives here — invoked by your intent, never running behind your back. The
      skills form a why-stack: each layer is checkable against the one below.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="grid cols2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>telos — what it's all for</h3>
          <p class="big">Build a purpose file from the evidence your machine can see, then
          get interrogated about it — hardest at the top.</p>
          <p>Objectives are derived and cited from your activity log and repos. Goals are
          proposed. The mission layer is <strong>never</strong> derived — a pattern mined
          from logs presented as your purpose would be a fumbled week laundered into an
          identity — so it's hypotheses only, stated by you or left honestly unresolved.
          Deliberately never injected into sessions; invoked as <code>/keel:telos</code>,
          and a work machine builds a work-scoped telos by construction.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>deck — what's promised</h3>
          <p class="big">Say a commitment out loud today; ask “where do I stand?” in a
          fresh session tomorrow.</p>
          <p>Captures promises in your own words — “review it deeply enough that it can
          merge by Wednesday” carries the priority a due-date field can't. At triage it
          verifies every pointer live (PR state, ticket status), pulls recent motion from
          the activity log, flags the promise with <em>no</em> next move, and lets you
          reorder in plain language. The note stores promises, never states — status is
          checked live, so it cannot go stale.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>week — what happened</h3>
          <p class="big">Reconstruct what you actually did, from three sources — because
          git alone under-reports your most valuable work.</p>
          <p>Evaluating options, reviewing someone else's change, investigating and
          concluding <em>no</em> — none of it leaves a commit. week merges the activity
          log, git across every checkout the log names, and PR/ticket servers where
          connected, then drafts the summary you can actually send. It never invents
          activity, and says “no evidence” rather than “no work.”</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>guide — how it all works</h3>
          <p class="big">keel explained from the live tree, never from memory of it.</p>
          <p>Reads the sibling skills' own files, runs the CLI's own help, and answers
          from what's actually on disk — so it covers every skill keel grows next with
          zero maintenance. Walkthrough mode runs a skill's read-only steps and stops
          where the writes would begin. Every explanation keeps the exit visible.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>setup — how it's wired</h3>
          <p class="big">The install ceremony, as a conversation.</p>
          <p>Inspects first, asks only about the gaps, names every write before making
          it, and drives the CLI's flag form so you never babysit a TTY prompt. Derives
          your project registry from the activity log and git remotes, asking only for
          what machinery can't know. Never destructive; safe to re-run any time.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>the cli underneath</h3>
          <p class="big"><code>keel status · log · doctor · setup · settings · update ·
          migrate · link</code></p>
          <p>Read-mostly, flags-first, idempotent writes only to keys it owns. It lives on
          the Bash tool's PATH rather than your shell's — in practice, Claude runs it for
          you. <code>keel doctor</code> is the noisy one: it verifies what everything else
          fails silently about, down to whether your memory adapter actually writes where
          it claims.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
